  "low_light_plants_title": "Low-light plants for apartments",
  "low_light_plants_article": "Try a ZZ plant, snake plant, or pothos in a room with a window but little direct sun. Low light still means daylight. If you cannot read comfortably there during the day, move the pot closer to a window.\n\nPlants in dim spots often use water slowly. Check the potting mix before watering, and empty any water that collects in the saucer.",
  "watering_plants_title": "How to tell when to water",
  "watering_plants_article": "Start by checking the potting mix with your finger. Many foliage plants are ready for water when the top layer feels dry. Let a succulent dry more thoroughly between waterings.\n\nWater evenly until it drains from the bottom, then empty the saucer. Check more often during hot spells and less often when the plant is growing slowly.",
  "balcony_styling_title": "Style a small balcony with plants",
  "balcony_styling_article": "Watch where the sun falls before picking plants. A balcony with hot afternoon sun needs different plants from one shaded all day. Note where wind hits hardest as well.\n\nGroup pots of different heights in one corner and keep the walkway clear. Choose stable containers with drainage, and secure anything you place near the railing.",
  "styling_plant_shelves_title": "Arrange plants on shelves",
  "styling_plant_shelves_article": "Put light-loving plants on the shelf with the brightest indirect light. Let a trailing pothos spill over one end and balance it with an upright plant on the other.\n\nUse saucers or waterproof trays to protect the shelf. Leave enough room to lift out each pot for watering, and keep heavier pots lower down.",
